{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVjuMqWBrVdvpr18b3tE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking about PostgreSQL advisory locks and what happens when:\n1. A session holding an advisory lock dies mid-transaction\n2. Whether the next waiter gets the lock immediately or has to wait for TCP keepalive\n\nThis is a straightforward technical question about PostgreSQL internals. They explicitly say they're not touching code yet and just want to understand the behavior. They want a direct, technical answer.\n\nI should not use memory or tools for this - it's a factual/technical question about PostgreSQL behavior that I can answer from my knowledge. Let me think through what I know:\n\n1. **Advisory locks in PostgreSQL**: These are application-level locks managed by the database, acquired via functions like `pg_advisory_lock()`, `pg_advisory_xact_lock()`, etc.\n\n2. **Session death mid-transaction**:\n   - If a session dies ungracefully (process crash, network disconnect), the connection is immediately detected as dead by the database\n   - PostgreSQL doesn't wait for TCP keepalive to detect this - it uses multiple mechanisms:\n     - The backend process managing that session will be terminated\n     - The session is cleaned up\n     - All locks held by that session are released\n\n3. **Advisory locks specifically**:\n   - Session-level advisory locks (acquired via `pg_advisory_lock()`) are held for the lifetime of the connection\n   - Transaction-level advisory locks (acquired via `pg_advisory_xact_lock()`) are held only for the transaction\n   - When a session dies/disconnects, ALL locks held by that session are immediately released\n   - This happens essentially immediately when the backend detects the connection is dead\n\n4. **Detection speed**:\n   - PostgreSQL uses a backend process per connection (on most systems)\n   - When a client disconnects ungracefully, the backend gets an EOF or error when trying to read from the socket\n   - This happens on the next attempt to read from the client\n   - Alternatively, the client-side can close the connection cleanly\n   - TCP keepalive is a fallback mechanism with much longer timeouts (typically 2 hours on many systems) and isn't what detects these failures\n\n5. **The practical answer**: When a session holding an advisory lock dies, the lock is released within milliseconds as the backend detects the dead connection and cleans up. Waiting processes are woken up immediately and can acquire the lock on their next attempt—TCP keepalive isn't involved in this process at all.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6845,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6845},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"546dd200-f137-47c5-b408-d43dccb0c726","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:45:53.974Z","request_id":"req_011CdVjuL4bg8QzQz343McQh"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVjuMqWBrVdvpr18b3tE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PostgreSQL **releases advisory locks immediately when the session dies**, not on TCP keepalive timeout. Here's the mechanics:\n\n**What happens:**\n1. Session crashes or network drops → client connection closes\n2. The backend process serving that session detects the EOF/error on the next socket read (usually immediate, within ms)\n3. Backend self-terminates and runs cleanup: all locks, transactions, temp tables held by that session are released\n4. Waiting processes are unblocked and can acquire the lock\n\n**The timing:**\n- **Not** TCP keepalive (that's 2+ hours by default, a fallback for truly hung connections)\n- **Not** polling—it's immediate detection when the backend gets a socket error\n- Practical latency: milliseconds, as soon as the backend's event loop gets a chance to read from that socket and see the error\n\n**Edge case to watch:** If the client process is *hung* (not actually dead, just paused—VM suspend, debugger breakpoint, etc), then:\n- The backend still sees a live connection\n- Advisory lock stays held\n- You actually *do* hit TCP keepalive behavior (or your app's connection timeout) before cleanup\n- This is rare but the failure mode to understand if you're worried about locked state\n\n**For your failure analysis:** If you're seeing hanging locks after a crash, the question is usually whether the client actually disconnected cleanly. If the connection cleanup isn't happening fast enough, check:\n- Whether the backend process is actually terminating (check pg_stat_activity)\n- Connection pooler behavior (might keep \"dead\" sessions alive)\n- Whether you're seeing the crash in the logs (backend got SIGTERM, or connection reset by peer)\n\nWhat's the failure you're investigating—clients reconnecting and still blocked on the lock?"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6845,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6845},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"2e8be8fb-acff-45da-be73-0f202e44e1eb","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:45:57.650Z","request_id":"req_011CdVjuL4bg8QzQz343McQh"}
